course: world
lesson: 10
title: Thinking like an astronomer
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: The Moon turns around its own axis exactly once while it goes around the Earth once, so it always keeps the same face towards us: walk around a chair while always facing it, and you will find you have turned around once yourself.
explain: Your mass, the amount of you, is the same everywhere, but your weight is the pull of gravity on you; the Moon has much less mass than the Earth, so it pulls about six times more weakly, and a child of 30 kilograms would weigh as much as 5 kilograms do on the Earth.
explain: The night sky is dark although there are countless stars, because the universe had a beginning: light from very distant stars has not had time to reach us, and so in most directions our sight runs out into darkness before it meets a star.
explain: The sky is blue by day because the air scatters the blue part of sunlight all over the sky; on the Moon, with no air, the sky is black even at noon and the stars can be seen beside the Sun.
explain: An astronomer cannot travel to the stars, so he reasons from what he sees: shadows, shapes, timings and the rules of light and gravity.
fact: The Moon turns once on its axis in the same time it takes to go once around the Earth.
fact: Because of this, we always see the same side of the Moon from the Earth.
fact: The far side of the Moon gets sunlight too; it is not always dark.
fact: Mass is the amount of matter in an object, and it is the same everywhere.
fact: Weight is the pull of gravity on an object, and it changes from place to place.
fact: The Moon's gravity is about one sixth of the Earth's gravity.
fact: On the Moon you could jump about six times higher than on the Earth.
fact: The Moon has no air, so its sky is black even in the daytime.
fact: The Earth's sky is blue because air scatters blue sunlight in all directions.
fact: The night sky is dark partly because light from very far stars has not yet had time to reach us.
fact: Stars are spread out very thinly, with huge empty spaces between them.
fact: On the Moon there is no wind or rain, so astronauts' footprints can last for millions of years.
exercise: Why do we always see the same face of the Moon? | It turns once on its axis in the time it goes once around the Earth | It does not turn on its axis at all | Its other side is too dark to see | The Earth's gravity holds it perfectly still so that it can never turn
exercise: Is the far side of the Moon always dark? | No, it gets sunlight when the side we see is dark | Yes, the Sun never shines on it | Yes, the Earth always shades it | No, it makes its own light
exercise: What is the difference between mass and weight? | Mass is how much matter; weight is gravity's pull on it | Mass is gravity's pull; weight is how much matter | They are two names for the same thing | Mass changes on the Moon, but weight stays just the same everywhere
exercise: A child has a mass of 30 kilograms on the Earth. What is her mass on the Moon? | Still 30 kilograms | Only 5 kilograms there | About 180 kilograms | Nothing at all, it's zero
exercise: Why would you weigh less on the Moon? | The Moon has less mass, so it pulls more weakly | The Moon has no gravity at all, so nothing pulls you | You would lose some of your body | The Moon's air holds you up
exercise: About how strong is the Moon's gravity compared with the Earth's? | About one sixth | About one half | About the same | About six times stronger
exercise: Why is the sky on the Moon black even in the daytime? | There is no air to scatter the sunlight | The Sun is far smaller seen from the Moon | The Moon's dust swallows all the light | It is always night on the Moon
exercise: Why is the daytime sky on the Earth blue? | Air scatters blue sunlight all over the sky | The sky reflects the colour of all the oceans | There is blue gas painted high up in the air | The Sun gives out only blue light around noon
exercise: Why can footprints on the Moon last for millions of years? | No wind or rain wipes them away | The Moon's dust is as hard as glass | The Moon's gravity glues them down | Astronauts walked very heavily
exercise: How high could you jump on the Moon compared with the Earth? | About six times higher | About the same height | About half as high | About sixty times higher
exercise: What is one reason the night sky is dark despite all the stars? | Light from very far stars has not reached us yet | Most stars switch off at night | The Moon blocks out the light of most of the stars | The Earth's air stops all starlight
exercise: How are the stars spread through space? | Very thinly, with huge empty spaces between them | Packed so tightly that they touch | All on one flat dome above the Earth | In one ring around the solar system
exercise: What could an astronaut see on the Moon at noon that we cannot see at noon? | Stars in a black sky beside the Sun | A blue sky with white fluffy clouds | A rainbow after the rain has stopped | Clouds drifting slowly across the Sun
exercise: When you walk around a chair always facing it, how many times do you turn around yourself? | Once | Never | Twice | Four times
test: If the Moon did not turn on its axis at all, what would we see from the Earth over a month? | All sides of the Moon in turn | Always the same face, as now | No Moon at all | Only the far side of the Moon
test: On the Earth a bathroom scale shows 30 kilograms for a girl. What would the same scale show on the Moon? | About 5 kilograms | About 30 kilograms | About 180 kilograms | About 0 kilograms
test: A boy says: «On the Moon I would be lighter because I would be smaller.» What is wrong? | He stays the same size; only the pull of gravity is weaker | Nothing is wrong; people really do shrink a little on the Moon | He would be heavier on the Moon | The Moon has stronger gravity than the Earth
test: Which is true? | A bag of rice has the same mass on the Earth and on the Moon | A bag of rice has more mass on the Moon | A bag of rice has the same weight everywhere | A bag of rice has no mass in space
test: A planet has no air at all. What colour is its sky in daylight? | Black | Blue | White | Red
test: Why is it not true that there is a «dark side of the Moon» that never sees the Sun? | Every side of the Moon faces the Sun at some time each month | The Moon makes its own light on the far side | The Earth lights up the whole far side with the glow of its big cities | Mirrors on the Moon reflect light there
test: If the universe had always existed with stars everywhere and light had forever to travel, what might the night sky look like? | Bright in every direction | Exactly as dark as it is now | Completely dark with no stars | Blue like the daytime sky
test: An astronaut on the Moon throws a ball as hard as on the Earth. Why does it fly much farther? | The weaker gravity pulls it down more slowly, and there is no air to slow it | The Moon's air pushes it forward | The ball becomes lighter in mass on the Moon, so the same throw sends it farther | The Moon spins faster under it
test: Which shows best that the Moon turns on its axis? | It keeps the same face to us while going all the way around us | It has phases every month | It rises in the east | It looks much bigger when it is low near the horizon than when high up
test: why do we only ever see one side of the moon | It turns once in the same time it goes round us | The other side is always in the dark | The Moon doesn't turn at all | The Earth blocks our view of the far side
test: if i jump on the moon how high can i go compared to here | About six times higher | About the same height | About two times higher | About a hundred times higher
test: my brother says the far side of the moon is always dark, it's the "dark side". true? | No, it gets sunlight too, we just never see it | Yes, the Sun never shines on it | Yes, it's dark because it faces away from Earth | No, it's lit only at full moon
test: Mia reckons you'd weigh less on the moon cos your mass goes down. right? | No, your mass stays the same; the Moon pulls less | Yes, you lose some of your mass when you go into space | Yes, both your mass and your weight halve up there | No, you'd weigh more there because the Moon is smaller
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the same face of the Moon is perpetually presented to the Earth. | Its rotation and its revolution about the Earth occupy equal periods | It does not rotate upon its axis at all | Its far side is permanently in darkness | The Earth's gravity prevents it from turning
exam: It is given that an object possesses a mass of 60 kilograms upon the Earth. Ascertain its mass upon the Moon. | Sixty kilograms | Ten kilograms | Three hundred and sixty kilograms | Zero kilograms
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the lunar sky appears black even during the lunar day. | The Moon possesses no atmosphere to scatter sunlight | The Sun does not illuminate the surface of the Moon at all | The lunar dust absorbs the whole of the light that falls upon it | The Earth obstructs the sunlight before it reaches the Moon
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the far side of the Moon. | It receives sunlight in the same manner as the near side | It remains in perpetual darkness | It is illuminated solely by the Earth | It emits a light of its own
